// std.core.drop
//
// RAII. The compiler calls `drop` when a binding leaves scope, in
// reverse declaration order, exactly once, after the block's own
// defers (a defer belongs to the block, a drop belongs to the value).
// Exactly-once is why `consume` exists: a Drop value can't be copied,
// since both copies would drop. Want a second one? Construct one —
// there is no Clone.
//
// Two consequences that look like they bite and do not:
//   - a handle is not a Drop value. Alloc is a fat value pointing at
//     an arena, so it copies freely; the ARENA is Drop. That's why
//     Vec can store `alloc: Alloc` by value.
//   - passing a Drop value to a parameter is a borrow, not a move —
//     a receiver is just the first parameter. A move is spelled
//     `consume`, at the call site only.
//
// Flagship user: env.mem.alloc() hands back an arena; dropping it
// releases every page it ever handed out, in one shot. Nothing checks
// any of this until stage 3; the stdlib honours exactly-once by hand.
// `consume` ships unchecked at stage 0 for that reason: code without
// it needs revision, code with it loses nothing.
Drop* = {
    // required (`= sig`, no body): an impl must provide it.
    //
    // `::` because drop writes the receiver's own bytes. The
    // compiler-inserted call is exempt from the receiver rule —
    // scope exit runs drop on `:` bindings too, because destroying
    // a value is not mutating it through a binding.
    drop* = (self :: @Self) ()
}
